  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints **27.06**, and all 7 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, so `\"12.5\"` became `12` and `\"05\"`, which is 1205 cents instead of 1250. That gave a subtotal of 24.10 plus 8.25% tax = 26.09. Inputs with exactly two decimals (`12.34`) or none (`12`) were unaffected, which is why the existing tests didn't catch it.\n\n**Fix:** changed it to `int(frac.ljust(2, \"0\"))`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. I also removed the `or 0`, which never did anything because the padded string is never empty. Now 12.50 \u00d7 2 = 25.00, plus 2.06 tax, comes to 27.06.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_money.py`, which covers one decimal, two decimals, no decimals and `0.05`.\n\nI didn't fix one related bug: negative amounts are wrong too. `to_cents(\"-1.50\")` returns -50 instead of -150, because the fraction is added to a negative whole number. The CLI probably never receives negative prices, but tell me if you want that handled as well.",
